    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is actually to *not* disable the interface (by default, Juniper interfaces are enabled). However, if you specifically want to represent the \"no shutdown\" (enable) command in Juniper syntax, you would *remove* any disable statement. Since the question asks to translate \"no shutdown\" directly, the closest positive action would be to ensure the interface isn\\'t disabled, but Juniper doesn\\'t have a direct \"enable\" command - interfaces are enabled by default unless explicitly disabled. \\n\\nA more accurate translation would be the *absence* of a disable command, but since the prompt requires a command output, the above shows how you would undo a shutdown state in Juniper (if the interface was previously disabled).', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable'}]]": 1,
    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable false', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable false\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, \"no shutdown\" is equivalent to enabling the interface, which is achieved by setting \"disable\" to \"false\". The interface naming convention also differs slightly (ge-x/x/x in Juniper vs GigabitEthernetx/x/x in Cisco). This translation assumes you want the equivalent for interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1. The same command structure would apply to other interfaces by changing the interface name (e.g., ge-1/0/2, ge-1/0/3).', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable false'}]]": 1,
